<div>
   <section> =20
   	  <a name=3D"Notes"></a>
      <h2>
         Notes=20
      </h2>    =20

		  <h3>
			 What fonts are installed and what does each one look like?=20
		  </h3>=09
		 =20
			<p>
				When you open the download document, Word_FontList_s4p.docm,
				there are notes and instructions to run aFONTLIST_s4p.
				<i>
				The code is open, not protected,=20
				so you can learn from it,
				customize it,
				and give me modified VBA to make it better for everyone.
				</i>
			</p>

			<p>			=09
		=09
				Run=20
				<b>
					aFONTLIST_s4p
				</b>
				(Alt-F8 for Macros list).
				The name starts with 'a' so should be at the top of the list.
			</p>

			<p>			=09
			=09
				A Word document is created with a list of all the fonts installed on yo=
ur computer,=20
				and examples of what characters look like in each.
				That's the real reason for this tool.
				By default, to keep it short but long enough,
				the example for each font is common keyboard characters with
				uppercase letters,
				lowercase letters, digits, punctation, and some special characters.=09
			</p>

			<p>=09
				Run the procedure without changing anything and=20
				within a minute or so,=20
				you'll get a great list,
				maybe even worthy of printing and using for wall paper.			=09
			</p>

			<p>=09
				Look in the lower left of the Word document window
					as the code is running to read the status bar
					and see where it's at.
					VBA turns on the Status Bar in Word and writes messages.
					<i>
						It also leaves the status bar showing but clears the message.
					</i>
			</p>

			<p>
				In aFONTLIST_s4p, you can modify the variables that set parameters that=
 are sent to FontList_s4p_Word,
				which is what creates the document and its contents, =20
				for different outputs.	=09
			</p>
=09
		=09
		  <h4>
			 Variables
		  </h4>			=09
		=09
			<p>
				Variables you can set.
				All are optional and have a default if not specified.
			</p>
			<p>
		=09
				<table cellspacing=3D"7">
				<tbody><tr>
					<th align=3D"left" valign=3D"top">Variable &nbsp; </th>
					<th align=3D"left" valign=3D"top">Description &nbsp; </th>		=09
				</tr> 			=09
					<tr>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   nEndExample As Long
						</td>
						<td valign=3D"top">=20
						   choose characters to show
						   <ul>=20
						   <li> 0 (default) =3D Common keyboard characters
						   </li><li> 1 =3D ASCII
						   </li><li> 2 =3D ASCII and Extended ASCII
						   </li><li> =E2=89=A5 32 shows Unicode characters for decimal value =
32 and above using ChrW()=20
						   ending with whatever is specified
						   </li></ul>
						=20
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   bSort As Boolean
						</td>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   True to sort by font name when done.
						   Default =3D False.
						</td>
					</tr>				=09
					<tr>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   bWatch As Boolean
						</td>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   True to watch as Word document is written =20
						   (takes more time but is sometimes necessary to ensure better accur=
acy marking the font=20
						   if you're listing several hundred characters in each table cell.=
=20
						   However, if the specified font doesn't have characters defined for=
 a point specified,
						   then it seems that Word will sometimes automatically choose anothe=
r font).
						   Default =3D False.
						</td>
					</tr>=09
					<tr>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   sFontname As String
						</td>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   specify to limit output to only one font
						</td>
					</tr>					=09
					<tr>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   sFontlist As String
						</td>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   specify to limit output to a list of fonts
						</td>
					</tr>	=09
					<tr>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   sNameDescription As String
						</td>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   text to include in filename
						</td>
					</tr>=09
					<tr>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   nStartExample As Long
						</td>
						<td valign=3D"top">
						   start point for Unicode characters to show. Under 32 not allowed.
						   Default is zero or not set, and 32 will be used.
						</td>
					</tr>=09

				=09
				</tbody></table>

			</p>=09

		<p> =20
			Instead of listing font names yourself (sFontlist),
			you can specify a wildcard pattern (sWildPattern)
			for Word to build a list of font names=20
			that match a pattern such as *What* where What is what you want to match=
=20
			like all font names that include
			Aptos, Calibri, Arial, Verdana, Script, Bold, ding, or Mono in the name.
		</p>
		=09
		=09
		<h3>
			Additional procedures=20
		</h3>		=09
		<h4>
			WhatIsSelectedCharacter()=20
		</h4>=09
=09

		<p> =20
			When you're looking at a document, any document,
			you can select a character=20
			and run=20
			WhatIsSelectedCharacter()=20
			to get information
			about the (first) character selected.=20
		</p>
	=09
		<p>		=09
			This procedure reads the current selection in Word and=20
			gives information in a message box.
			It shows the character*,=20
			and reports the results of
			Asc()
			and
			AscW()
			on the rendered character.
			Code makes an adjustment if a negative number is returned for ChrW()
			since only positive numbers were used to create the characters.
			Negative numbers are okay for ChrW but you can't enter them using your k=
eyboard=20
			(or if you can, I don't know how).
		=09
		</p>	=09
	=09
		<p>	 =20
			*Displaying a message to the user showing the selected character =20
			uses Daniel Pineault's=20
		<i>
			Unicode_MsgBox
		</i> function

		to show Unicode better=20
		~ =E2=80=94 although that's not perfect, but better.

		</p> =09

		=09
		<p>
			However, even though a message box that can show Unicode is used
				as opposed to the standard Access MsgBox,
			the message font isn't the same as whatever you're selecting
			so maybe the representation isn't right either.
		</p>
		=09
	  <h4>
		 InputCharGetAsc()=20
	  </h4>		=09
		<p>
			Get numeric return from the Asc() function for a character you type=20
			on the keyboard when prompted.
		</p>		=09

		<p>
			When you press a key on your keyboard, a numeric code is sent to the pro=
cessor,
			not the actual character printed on the key.
			On a standard keyboard, when you press capital A, 65 is sent whereas
			when you press little a, 97 is sent.	=09
		</p>		=09
		=09
		  <h2>
			 Character encoding=20
		  </h2>=09
			<p>
				Character encoding defines how numbers correspond to a visual represent=
ation.
				Each number is called a code point.
				Some points include actions.
				ASCII and UTF-8 are different encoding methods.

			</p>

		=09
		  <h3>
			 ASCII=20
		  </h3>
	=09
			<p>
				ASCII stands for the "American Standard Code for Information Interchang=
e".
				It was designed decades ago so different systems =20
				could exchange files.
				Other conventions are often built on ASCII.
			</p>		=09
			<p>=09
				The ASCII set contains 128 characters,
				some of which also are used for device control=20
				  ... from 0 to 127,
				which can be represented with 7 bits=20
				<br>
				64+32+16+8+4+2+1=3D127			=09
			</p>		=09
			<p>		=09
				The points below 32 are for device control such as
				Line Feed, Carriage Return, Line Feed, Bell, and Escape.			=09
			</p>		=09
			<p>
				32 is a space character.
				Then come numbers, uppercase letters lowercase letters,
				with punctuation and special characters in between.
			</p>=09
			<h3>
				ANSI=20
			</h3>
			<p>=09
				It seems that ANSI has no standard specification that everyone agreed o=
n.=09
				There are different conventions.			=09
			</p>
		=09
=09
		  <h3>
			 Extended ASCII=20
		  </h3>
			<p>=09
				Extended ASCII adds 1 more bit than ASCII=20
				so possibities add the range from 128 to 255.
				<br>
				127+128=3D255		=09
			</p>
			<p>=09
				While ASCII was pretty much adopted,=20
				the Extended ASCII set was in dispute.=20
				Many languages wanted to add characters that English doesn't use,
				and different people had different ideas.
				And 128 more characters really wasn't much.
			</p>		=09
			<p>
				There needed to be more, many more, standard universal characters.
				That's where Unicode steps in.

			</p>=09

		=09
		  <h3>
			 Unicode=20
		  </h3>
			<p>=09
				Unicode is separated into named blocks,=20
				each containing a varying number of points.
				A point defines the visual symbol for a number.
				The first block is based on ASCII.
			</p>		=09
			<p>	=09
				There are multiple ways of storing (encoding) Unicode
			</p>		=09
		  <h4>
			 UTF-8
		  </h4>
			<p>				=09

				UTF stands for Unicode Transformation Format,
				and the 8 means 8-bit,=20
				which is the minimum size since it can grow.
				UTF-8 can string on more bytes as needed.=20
				Because it sends less down the wire,=20
				it is the method usually used to transmit webpages.

			</p>			=09
			<p>
		=09
				The idea for UTF-8 was written on a napkin in a diner.
				It was designed for backward compatibility with ASCII
				and set up so that characters would use minimum space to store.
			</p>=09
			<p>			=09
				UTF-8 is one of the ways that Unicode can be encoded,
				and only uses bytes beyond the first as needed.
				Other forms of Unicode have set sizes.=20
				UTF-16 is fixed at 2 bytes per code point.
				UTF-32 is fixed at 4 bytes per code point.
			</p>

		=09
		  <h4>
			 Unicode
		  </h4>			=09
			=09
			<p>
		=09
				A whole lot can be said about Unicode.
				Suffice it to say, there are a lot of characters you can look up and us=
e.=09
				A point is a number that corresponds to one symbol and=20
				appears or not=20
				depending on what font is being used and what's defined.
			</p>			=09
			<p>			=09
				Some fonts are specifically designed for Unicode=20
				like Lucida Sans Unicode
				and have more points defined than average.
			=09
			=09
				Common fonts are good choices for more points,
				like Arial, Times New Roman, Calibri, and perhaps Aptos.			=09
			</p>		 =09
	=09
	=09
	  <h2>
		 VBA functions to switch between number and character=20
	  </h2>		=09
	=09
		  <h3>
			 Asc() function=20
		  </h3>		=09
			<p>		=09
				The Asc() function is short for ASCII=20
				although what it returns may be ANSI (also loosely defined).=20
				There was a lot of dispute over what the 'entended' range should be.
				Given a character, the Asc() function returns its number.
			</p>	=09
	=09
		  <h3>
			 AscW() function=20
		  </h3>			 =20
			<p>		=09
				The AscW() function is a wide version of Asc() to include some of Unico=
de.
			</p>
		=09
		  <h3>
			 Chr() function=20
		  </h3>	=09
			<p>=09
				The Chr() function is used to convert a number into a character.
			</p>=09

		=09
		  <h3>
			 ChrW() function=20
		  </h3>					=09
			<p>			=09
				ChrW() is the wide version to return a Unicode character; and
				you may need to change the display font to show it properly.
			</p>		=09
					=09
		<h3>
			 function notes
		 </h3>
		 =20
			<p>
				Some fonts render characters defined in the
				128-255 range as generated by the Chr() function,
				and decrypted using Asc()
				but they don't compare to the Unicode points for that font =E2=80=94
				mostly not anyway, or not at all.=09
				You can see this if you compare the Extended ASCII output
				vs Unicode to 255 in the FontList documents.
			</p>		=09
			<p>
				You can use the WhatIsSelectedCharacter sub to find out=20
				the Chr() and ChrW value for a character.
			</p>					=09

	</section>
