<div>
   <section> =20
   	  <a name=3D"Backstory"></a>
      <h2>
         Back Story=20
      </h2> =20
      <p>
		In the early days, when there weren't so many fonts,
		I loved the posters to hang on your wall
		with a line of characters for each face.
		That was the drive for this tool.
		But now there are a thousand fonts installed on my computer ... what do t=
hey look like?=09
		And how do I limit the list to the ones I want to focus on?
		I spent a lot of time testing for different parameters,
		and speeding up the code.	=09
      </p> 	 =20
	 =20
      <p>
	 =20
		Word is the best tool to find out with!
		That should be no surprise as=20
		fonts are used to show words,	=09
		and Word has a FontNames collection
		that you can read with VBA.

      </p> 	 =20
	 =20
      <p>=09
		Seeing what fonts (typefaces) look like
		helps you choose what you want to use =20
		for the meaning and feeling that you want to convey.
      </p> =09
      <p>=09
	=09
		From the documents that are created,=20
		you can copy a character or characters =20
		to use somewhere else.	 =20
      </p> =09
      <p>=09
			If characters are wide, you'll see more than a couple lines for a font i=
n the list.
			If Word wrapped a paragraph, there's a hanging indent=20
			so the first line is on the margin and subsequent lines are indented a l=
ittle.
			Depending on the text example, code adds line breaks to make it easier=
=20
			to compare 2 different fonts and keep your place.
			When specifying Unicode, paragraph markers are inserted=20
			before each multiple of 128.						=09
      </p> =09
 =09
      <p>

		 The WhatIsSelectedCharacter sub
		 determines numbers using Asc() and AscW()
		 and shows a message box using Unicode to show the character=20
		 using Daniel Pineault's Unicode_MsgBox code.=20
      </p> =09
 =09
      <p>		 		=20
		 The InputCharGetAsc sub is handy for figuring out how your keyboard is m=
apped
		 and the scan code it sends.=20

		</p>	=20
	 =20
		<p>
			Now that the parameters I want for myself are working
			to generate lists of fonts and examples,
			and I can get information about what's selected,
			as well as press a key on the keyboard and find out its scan code,
			there is more that could be done. 		=09
      </p>	=20
	 =20
	  <ul>
		<li>
			It would be nice to have a way to save characters/symbols
			that you like and want to use again,
			as well as mark fonts you're interested in
			(database?).
		</li>	 =20
		<li>
			Make a way to report numbers in the message box based on=20
			the source number for what was generated
			instead of what is rendered.
			Word does this funny thing about substituting other fonts
			than what is specified
			when certain Unicode characters are input,
			which makes it hard to get an accurate view of just what is defined by t=
he font.
		=09
			Oh, I guess this would be another reason to use Access to automate Word =
....
		</li>=09
	 =20
		<li>
			Another handy thing with the FontNames collection you easily can get in =
Word,=20
			would be to store the available font names in Access=20
			for different computers so it's easy for a developer to use the ones the=
y all have
			when deviating from standard fonts for captions and format codes.
		</li>	 =20
		<li>		=09
			CHALLENGE: to make this an easier tool for others,
			it would be good to make a userform instead of having to modify the code=
 for parameters to send=20
			... but I didn't do that.
			Maybe one of you reading this will ... and share back for everyone.
		</li>
		</ul>
	=09
		<i>	=09
		<p>=09

			Thanks to the many great Word developers I've learned from including
			Shauna Kelly, Graham Mayor, Greg Maxey, Graham Skan, Cindy Meister, Dian=
e Poremsky,
			Helen Feddema,
			and Doug Robbins.
		</p>	=20
		<p>
			Also to Access developers Adrian Bell for giving me more understanding a=
bout bits and bytes,
			and Daniel Pineault for sharing so many clever solutions.		=09
		</p>

		<p>
			~ always learning,=20
			<br>
			crystal
		</p>	=09
		</i>		=09

=20
     </section>
